<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frank Lloyd Wright</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frank Lloyd Wright was an American architect, designer, writer and educator who designed more than 1,000 structures over a creative period of 70 years. Raised in rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>, he apprenticed in Chicago with Louis Sullivan at Adler &amp; Sullivan, opened his own practice in 1893 and became a pioneer of the Prairie School movement. He called his philosophy of designing in harmony with humanity and the environment organic architecture, exemplified in Fallingwater (1935), and developed the Usonian home within Broadacre City, his vision for urban planning. In 2019 a selection of his work became a World Heritage Site as The 20th-Century Architecture of Frank Lloyd Wright.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frank-lloyd-wright.jpg" alt="Frank Lloyd Wright" width="330" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: New York World-Telegram and the Sun staff photographer: Al Ravenna · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frank_Lloyd_Wright_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bertolt Brecht, Eugene O’Neill and later Samuel Beckett shaped the theatre, and <b>Frank Lloyd Wright</b>, Le Corbusier and Ludwig Mies van der Rohe its architecture.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · People</a></li></ul></section>
